Emacsvox News -- History Of User-Visible Changes

Maintained by Emacsvox Contributors.

* Emacsvox Unreleased --- Current User-Visible Changes

Released notes are preserved in [[file:NEWS-2026.9.2][NEWS for Emacsvox 2026.9.2]].

** Back out of an engine's voice list one step at a time

After ~RET~ opens an engine's voices, ~q~ returns to that engine's row and
column.  Another ~q~ leaves the Workbench.  Returning works for empty or
refreshed voice lists and preserves unsaved routing changes; leaving the
Workbench still warns about those changes.

** Engine availability is distinct from routing policy

Browse and try voices reports engine availability before routing preferences.
The former /enabled/ label now says /Routing policy: allowed/; missing runtimes
remain /unavailable/ with their discovery reason visible in the row.

** Engine module operations show their progress

Downloadable modules say /not installed/ until installation starts.  The
module manager shows and speaks /installing/, /uninstalling/, or /testing/
while an operation runs, including after refresh or reopening.  Completion
and failure refresh the state, and reopening preserves the active operation
and its protection against starting another one.
The /Release state/ column describes the managed release; Browse and try
voices reflects the active server, which may use a different installation.

** Remote Emacs can use workstation speech

Development builds can connect remote Emacs to Omnivox on the workstation
through an authenticated SSH reverse forward.  Engines and audio remain on
the workstation; foreground and notification connections recover separately
without replaying unfinished speech.  Use ~omnivox-remote-connect~,
~omnivox-remote-status~, and ~omnivox-remote-disconnect~ with the endpoint and
private token options described in Speech Backends.  Windows services require
Windows OpenSSH for the forward when WSL uses NAT.  Omnivox 1.7.1 release
binaries predate this feature.

** Aural Home retains context and unfinished work

~C-e H~ remembers the source item as well as its buffer.  Moving the source
cursor does not retarget explanation or remapping; ~C-c C-o~ in Home returns
to the captured item.  Reopen ~C-e H~ from an ordinary buffer to adopt a new
target.  Help and Home retain their return context and selected row.

Buffer rule editors preserve separate drafts for each source buffer, and
reopening an editor or the same tuned voice preserves unfinished changes.
Generated current-item remaps recheck their source before applying.  Current
explanation and remapping no longer use unrelated earlier feedback; retained
historical presentations remain available through Recent Feedback.

** Consistent Aural navigation and actions

Settings lists speak the name and state together when moving vertically.
Child managers retain their row and column on reopening; Help returns to
its exact origin.  ~C-c C-a~ offers named actions and ~S~ stops previews.
The rule editor now uses ~n/p~ for movement, ~N~ for Add and ~P~ for Preview.
~RET~ opens details or a child list; profiles are applied with ~a~.
Palette ~P~ auditions its voices, while ~RET~ browses them.  Stopping or
interrupting an exact-voice sequence prevents late callbacks restarting it.

** Task groups and searchable destinations in Aural Home

Home begins with five expandable task groups.  ~RET~ or ~TAB~ expands a
group, and ~/~ searches every named action, including collapsed groups.
Existing direct shortcuts remain available.  Home announces the captured
source and unfinished draft count; Resume unfinished changes opens the
original editor.  Voice browsing, speech rate, engine preference, and
notification controls are available beside the advanced managers.

** Browse, identify, and compare physical voices

~RET~ on a Workbench engine opens its filtered voices; ~RET~ on a voice shows
details.  ~P~ plays a named sample.  ~A~ follows displayed order and skips
unavailable voices.  ~B~ starts with the selected voice and offers a filtered
second voice, with an explicit Search all engines choice.  Sample labels are
configurable through ~emacsvox-aural-preview-label-verbosity~.  Views retain
their own selected rows and columns, and fresh inventory updates open lists.
Exact-preview failures stop the sequence and report the requested voice's
failure instead of silently accepting another engine or voice.

** Temporary physical voice tuning and reviewed Keep result

Press ~t~ on a physical voice to experiment with its exact route, engine
parameters, and audio effects.  Navigation and Help use the working voice.
Compare opening and working versions, copy a style, switch voices, or hear
three parameter values without saving.  ~w~ chooses what to keep and opens a
review; ~C-c C-c~ in that review saves and applies the selected destinations.
Style and route stores remain separate, and incomplete saves or failed
application retain their completed steps for retry.  Requested settings and
reported playback support are shown separately; exact effective values are
not claimed when the adapter does not report them.

** Guided changes to selected feedback

Home ~c~ opens Change this feedback for Current item; Recent Feedback ~C~
uses the selected frozen record and asks for a part when needed.  Change a
voice, add or replace a cue or tone, add a spoken label, or suppress one
component.  ~P~ previews before choosing persistence, ~O~ replays the original,
~m~ chooses matching criteria, and ~l~ chooses lifetime.  Review the summary
before ~a~ or ~C-c C-c~ applies or saves.  ~e~ opens the Advanced rule editor;
~q~ retains the unfinished draft.  Matching breadth and lifetime are described
separately, and failures preserve recoverable work.

** Task recipes and offline help

Aural interfaces offer ~C-c C-i~ for their offline task manual, with ~q~
returning to the exact origin; Help also offers ~i~ and a manual link.  Worked
recipes cover physical voices, pitch comparisons, Keep result, heading
voices, cues, blank-line tones, engine preference, and draft recovery.
Home Help starts with tasks.  Training mode ~RET~ shows details and ~t~
explicitly toggles explanations for the session.  Tuner value prompts now
state when blank retains the current value, and option Actions hide operations
that cannot apply to the selected row.
